    "hoursAgo": 23,
    "datePosted": "2026-11-12T13:49:31.000Z",
    "description": "CALL FOR PAPERS From Representation to Influence: Advancing Women’s Leadership and Decision-Making in Ethiopia’s Post-Election Context Network of Ethiopian Women’s Associations (NEWA) in collaboration with UN Women Ethiopia, invites you to submit an abstract for presentation at a national conference to be held on 24 December 2026 (full day) and 25 December 2026 (half day) in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, under the theme “From Representation to Influence: Advancing Women’s Leadership and Decision-Making in Ethiopia’s Post-Election Context.” The conference aims to provide a multi-stakeholder platform for policymakers, leaders from public institutions and the private sector, researchers, women's rights organizations, civil society, media, development partners, and individual gender advocates to discuss how women's leadership can be advanced across all sectors and at all levels. Building on the window of opportunity created in the post-election period, including the formation of new government structures, and on the implementation of the National Women's Empowerment and Gender Equality Policy (2026), participants will take stock of the status and influence of women in leadership and decision-making roles, identify key priorities, and formulate practical recommendations for strengthening women's leadership and meaningful participation in decision-making. To inform and enrich the conference dialogue, researchers, academics, policymakers, and practitioners working on leadership and governance, gender equality, and women's empowerment are invited to submit original research papers, discussion papers, or case studies on advancing women's leadership and decision-making across sectors and at all levels, in light of the opportunities created in Ethiopia's post-election period. Abstracts should focus on one of the following thematic tracks: Track 1: Women in the electoral process Experiences of women as voters, candidates, party members, election administrators, journalists and observers. Electoral laws, quotas and temporary special measures, and their effect on women’s participation. Violence against women in elections (VAWiE) and in politics beyond elections (VAWiP), including online violence; prevention and response. Gender equality norms and structures in political parties, party manifestos and candidate selection. Track 2: Women’s Leadership in Legislative and Executive Bodies Women’s representation, leadership and substantive influence in the parliament, regional and city councils . Women’s leadership in federal and regional cabinets and zonal and woreda administrations: including pathways to leadership, progression and retention. Women’s influence in legislative, and governance processes, including law making, standing committees, budgeting and oversight and the role of women legislators, and parliamentary caucuses in advancing gender equality Track 3: Women’s Leadership in the Judiciary, Criminal Justice System, Peace and Security Women’s representation, leadership and influence in criminal justice institutions, in courts, prosecution, police and defence. Women’s leadership, participation and influence in peace-building, conflict prevention mediation and post-conflict recovery. Track 4: Leadership across sectors Women’s leadership in the civil service, private sector, academia, civil society and media. Media representation and portrayal of women leaders and its influence on perceptions of women’s leadership Track 5: Cross-cutting issues Social norms: Addressing the discriminatory social and gender norms and stereotypes that limit women's leadership aspirations, acceptance and influence, and restrict their participation in decision-making at household, community, and institutional levels. Unpaid care work: Addressing the disproportionate burden of unpaid care and domestic work that limits women's time, mobility, and opportunities to take up and sustain leadership and decision-making roles. Intersectionality: Addressing the compounding barriers to leadership and decision-making faced by young women, women with disabilities, women from rural and pastoralist communities, and conflict-affected and displaced women. Accountability and institutional mechanisms: Strengthening accountability and institutional mechanisms for advancing women's leadership and decision-making, including the opportunities and pathways that can be created through the implementation of the new National Women's Empowerment and Gender Equality Policy (2026) of Ethiopia.",
